\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Bulgaria \highlight\uc2  lev/dlr firms on ECB  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  fears. \b0\par\par\uc2 187 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2000\par 16:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SOFIA, Nov 10 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Bulgaria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lev gained against the dollar at Friday's close, reflecting euro's rise as markets feared the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in again to support the euro after three \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past week. \par \par \uc2  "Trade was mainly client-driven today with companies mostly buying dollars, but some banks remained sidelined wary of a new \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The lev ended the week at 2.245/2.247 per dollar, from 2.271/2.172 on Thursday, reflecting euro's rebound against the dollar on global markets through the lev/euro peg. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the ECB stepped into global markets to sell both dollars and yen to prop up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the third unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past week. \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the lev ended at 1.9463/1.9466, from 1.9461/1.9464 on Thursday as banks covered the rise in client dollar demand through euro/dollar arbitrage deals. \par \par \uc2  Bulgaria pegged the lev at 1.95583 per euro from 1999 as part of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwba028g6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
