\par\par \b \uc2  BULGARIA LEV CLOSES DOWN 37 PCT FROM END-1996. \b0\par\par\uc2 301 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Januar 1997\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SOFIA, Jan 4 (Reuter) - The Bulgarian lev lost 37 percent in the first two trading days of 1997 for the local interbank market, closing at a new record low of 695-698 per dollar on Saturday in extremely thin volume, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The lev opened at 620-660 per dollar and traded at 640-650 at midday, down from 590-605 at Friday's close and from 507-510 on Monday, the last trading day of 1996 for the local market before the New Year recess. \par \par \uc2  "Big final clients bid for large foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volumes while banks and clients alike were reluctant to sell amid an ongoing lev slide," a commercial bank dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "Final clients bought every dollar offered irrespective of the high price," another dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not seen selling dollars to support the crumbling lev, traders said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials were not available for comment. \par \par \uc2  Markets were closed in Bulgaria from Tuesday through Thursday for the New Year. \par \par \uc2  Bulgarian National Bank (BNB) governor Lyubomir Filipov said on Sunday that BNB powers to counter pressure on the lev through \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  were limited because payments on \b \highlight22\uc2 Bulgaria\b0 \highlight\uc2 's foreign debt in 1997 would be a priority for the BNB. \par \par \uc2  Low BNB \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, lack of financing from abroad and lower than expected privatisation proceeds in 1996 were the reasons why the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could not prevent the lev decline, Filipov said. \par \par \uc2  BNB \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at $525 million at the end of November last year, down from $1.408 billion a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  Bulgaria has to meet some $300 million of foreign debt payments in the first quarter with $170-180 million due in January alone. -- Anastas Petrov, Sofia Newsroom, 359-2-876032 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020040130dt1400cxa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
