\par\par \b \uc2  Brady Bond Market: Russia Rises On Bank Meeting, Polls \b0\par\par\uc2 339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 1996\par 13:34\par \uc2  Emerging Markets Report \par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  LONDON (Dow Jones)--Russian loans continued to rise on the market for developing country debt Wednesday, pushed higher by news of a meeting between the country's debt negotiators and its foreign bank creditors. \par \par \uc2  Recent improvements in President Boris Yeltsin's standing in the polls have also helped, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Vneshekonombank U.S. dollar loans gained one point on the offer side to 38.5 on news that a Russian delegation will meet with the country's Bank Advisory Committee (BAC) in London next Monday. The meeting is expected to produce a term sheet outlining the precise details of the debt rescheduling agreement negotiated in November. \par \par \uc2  Once agreed, the term sheet will be presented to Russia's 400 creditor banks for their approval, a significant step towards completing the agreement. \par \par \uc2  Recent polls which have shown Yeltsin gaining ground on Gennady Zyuganov, his main rival in June's presidential elections, have also helped boost Russian debt prices. \par \par \uc2  One poll published Monday gave Yeltsin a slight lead over the Communist Party candidate. It was the first nationwide poll to do so. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere in eastern Europe, Bulgarian Brady bonds edged slightly higher on hopes that the country will soon conclude agreements with the International Monetary Fund and the World Bank which will improve its ability to repay its foreign debt. \par \par \uc2  Bulgarian Discount bonds rose by 1/8 to 51.875 bid. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off the continuing decline in the rate of exchange of the Lev against the dollar. Dealers in Sofia said the National Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Bulgaria\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unable to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in support of the Lev because it doesn't have enough \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves stood at $700 million at the end of March. \par \par \uc2  Polish Discount bonds were down 1/8 at 92.75/93.00. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading of Latin American Brady bonds was limited, with Brazilian\par Capitalization Bonds down 1/8 at 60.25.\par    -Paul Hannon\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) DOW JONES EMERGING MARKETS REPORT 04-24-96\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0734EDT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1810 \par \par \uc2 Dokument em00000020011013ds4o007un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
